		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-39ecbe02-4055-4319-85af-5d0e64aedf9f">
		<rdfs:label>article-39ecbe02-4055-4319-85af-5d0e64aedf9f</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Census data showed that Boston’s population grew by 58,000 between the 2010 and 2020 counts, while the portion of residents identifying as white continued to dip, from 47 percent in 2010 to 44.5 percent.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-91caa243-6ff4-4c6d-affc-51bd8da8617d"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2021-08-12"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-39eee22c-2506-4954-b0dc-4d20f5799209">
		<rdfs:label>article-39eee22c-2506-4954-b0dc-4d20f5799209</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>New outbreaks used to be traced back to crowded factories and rowdy bars. But now, the virus is so widespread not even health officials are able to keep up.</rdfs:comment>
