	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-4815eb6c-31d0-4f9e-92af-0279764e9cb3">
		<rdfs:label>article-4815eb6c-31d0-4f9e-92af-0279764e9cb3</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The latest batch of mail ballots from Philadelphia pulled Joseph R. Biden Jr. ahead by more than 5,000 votes in Pennsylvania, putting him in a strong position to win a state that would lift him above the 270 Electoral College votes needed to win the presidency.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-42dfcd52-8183-4ae1-ba08-c61dc31db2c0"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-11-06"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-481718d4-5ec9-4574-9028-6fc53b194382">
		<rdfs:label>article-481718d4-5ec9-4574-9028-6fc53b194382</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Battleground states will use quite different methods to count the record number of mail-in ballots. But they may be the way of the future.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-21d856ad-a6ae-406e-a9e4-9707218ad815"/>
