	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-5514311b-aa72-4950-a194-ac2d58eb0780">
		<rdfs:label>article-5514311b-aa72-4950-a194-ac2d58eb0780</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>For ambitious Republicans who are mulling a presidential bid, a challenging and at times uncomfortable audition is underway this winter: trying to use the Trump political playbook to impress and inherit the former president’s supporters — all while navigating the limitation of not being Donald J. Trump. Last weekend’s annual Conservative Political Action Conference in Orlando, Fla., was the most visible stage yet for these politicians — not to mention where they confronted the most scrutiny.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-1a919c05-175d-4b8d-9183-278fc0c9195c"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2021-03-03"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-55196cad-8acd-452f-88f8-87fedad160c0">
