	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-5fa8a448-7acf-4877-b6ef-df7cd8b217f9">
		<rdfs:label>article-5fa8a448-7acf-4877-b6ef-df7cd8b217f9</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Since March 2020, millions of people in the United States with weakened immune systems, disabilities or illnesses that make them especially vulnerable to the coronavirus have been sequestering at home, keeping their children out of school and skipping medical care rather than risk exposure to the virus. And they have seethed over talk from politicians and public health experts that they perceive as minimizing the value of their lives.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-0a19ff37-a276-4081-944e-e43f9f3234c8"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-d3a2df06-dc48-4fab-a9d1-d1d12442e2e1"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2022-02-17"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-5fa9055c-c30c-4708-bd5d-ac0563425579">
		<rdfs:label>article-5fa9055c-c30c-4708-bd5d-ac0563425579</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Republicans fired off an array of political attacks, and Democrats got through their biggest hurdle to swearing in the first Black woman on the Supreme Court.</rdfs:comment>
