	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-729ecd1d-7b47-4e98-9cb5-a2e25d4fa4f1">
		<rdfs:label>article-729ecd1d-7b47-4e98-9cb5-a2e25d4fa4f1</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>What happens when working people are so vulnerable — without decent health care, child care and other forms of support — that it puts the whole city at risk?</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-03-16"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-72a1e3db-fc73-4feb-ae2b-cc5856629a67">
		<rdfs:label>article-72a1e3db-fc73-4feb-ae2b-cc5856629a67</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The number rose sharply from previous years, probably a result of more outreach, less stringent criteria for signing up, and the pandemic.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-99c0f96d-1b8c-4214-8727-29573022489d"/>
