	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-7a64aa9b-5db4-4639-bc9f-a0947a61fa4e">
		<rdfs:label>article-7a64aa9b-5db4-4639-bc9f-a0947a61fa4e</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Joe Biden’s supporters were dancing in the streets. But only half the nation was celebrating, and the nation’s divisions remain on stark display.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-6d7b9cf0-ac61-4722-8016-c030fa3458b0"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-11-09"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-7a6632ff-3253-40cd-9246-58434121cf77">
		<rdfs:label>article-7a6632ff-3253-40cd-9246-58434121cf77</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The current confrontation turns partly on what, if any, commitments Secretary of State James A. Baker III made about NATO’s expansion in the waning days of the Cold War.</rdfs:comment>
