		<rdfs:label>article-8817f219-a453-4487-8072-dce4ae8de093</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Anthony Fauci did not set out to become a political lightning rod. But, as Sheryl Gay Stolberg explains, he couldn’t escape becoming a polarizing figure in Donald Trump’s Washington.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2022-08-22"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-881a457f-1dd2-4bce-b0b5-bea22472d511">
		<rdfs:label>article-881a457f-1dd2-4bce-b0b5-bea22472d511</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Across the United States, coronavirus cases are surging and the Omicron variant is spreading, throwing the scheduling of schools, concerts and sports leagues into question. Just days away from a holiday weekend, Americans are also grappling with whether to change their traveling or gathering plans.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-078e57d0-5b20-48e9-b039-c2569b2acb05"/>
