	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-8d5ae2d4-12d3-489e-9a25-806ec8bbb67a">
		<rdfs:label>article-8d5ae2d4-12d3-489e-9a25-806ec8bbb67a</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Susan Rice, who was President Barack Obama’s national security adviser, will lead the Domestic Policy Council. Denis McDonough, Mr. Obama’s chief of staff, was nominated secretary of veterans affairs.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-fc2c2b47-32c2-43a0-8e5f-dd4e72335932"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-12-11"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-8d5b14b3-d7ef-438a-9fd4-5c2ec5e855b1">
		<rdfs:label>article-8d5b14b3-d7ef-438a-9fd4-5c2ec5e855b1</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Stumping for Elissa Slotkin in Michigan, Ms. Cheney had an urgent message for voters: “We all must stand and defend the republic.”</rdfs:comment>
