	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-a4efed43-5b74-46ef-b202-39fdbf6addda">
		<rdfs:label>article-a4efed43-5b74-46ef-b202-39fdbf6addda</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The Democratic National Convention, on the whole, was aimed more at appealing to moderates than rallying the base. Will the Republicans take the opposite approach?</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-a4504805-5b5f-41c7-b157-41ff04a342ad"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-c67a9047-7b94-4e45-aaa9-c820c2b967de"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-08-22"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-a4f1eab7-7e01-4449-800e-fd0f2530ff1c">
		<rdfs:label>article-a4f1eab7-7e01-4449-800e-fd0f2530ff1c</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Nearly two months after his unusually late entry into the presidential race, the former Massachusetts governor has failed to draw big headlines or crucial endorsements. But he still has a long-shot plan.</rdfs:comment>
