	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-ab4ab96c-a907-4f0c-b2e7-25a499f12f2d">
		<rdfs:label>article-ab4ab96c-a907-4f0c-b2e7-25a499f12f2d</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>PHOENIX — In the moonlight, dozens of people hollered and embraced, dropping the rules of social distancing as they celebrated a win after a long year: hundreds of thousands of Latino voters registered, calls made and doors knocked amid a pandemic that had devastated their communities. Though it would be days before the final result in Arizona was clear, the people working to shore up Latino support for Democrats in the state were already convinced that they helped shape history. They had come through the crucible of a pervasive anti-immigrant sentiment and a decade later, flipped the state and delivered wins.</rdfs:comment>
