	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-b442e05a-704f-4d8f-954a-7bd1b5d57447">
		<rdfs:label>article-b442e05a-704f-4d8f-954a-7bd1b5d57447</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The battle over whether Florida will go red or blue in the presidential election has been almost overshadowed by the state’s position on another color-coded map: Florida is listed in the red zone for coronavirus cases in the latest weekly report from the White House Coronavirus Task Force, which noted an increase in new cases over the past week.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-0b1c0734-81f6-4bd6-ab6e-2c3d3239bc31"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-5c7e1f74-a87d-4db2-a8c4-665fc5eb3d59"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-dad1523a-f983-4f0f-af53-0061c7165e91"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-10-29"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-b4472e3a-bed9-4bf3-8ffc-381a0983d90c">
		<rdfs:label>article-b4472e3a-bed9-4bf3-8ffc-381a0983d90c</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>In April, San Francisco residents used an average of 40 gallons of water per day, compared with 137 by those living in Riverside County.</rdfs:comment>
