		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-d2e8f9e6-beb7-4c55-b368-9b9202973afe">
		<rdfs:label>article-d2e8f9e6-beb7-4c55-b368-9b9202973afe</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>The decision runs counter to the views of the former president, who has argued that his decisions and deliberations are protected by executive privilege, and sets up a potential court battle.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-dd522e1c-57a0-49bc-9835-fae39d9d58a6"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2021-07-27"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-d2e972af-2ad0-4fe7-9dde-97f56bdaad80">
		<rdfs:label>article-d2e972af-2ad0-4fe7-9dde-97f56bdaad80</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>On Tuesday night, Republicans cast the president as the country’s greatest protector — not only from mobs of protesters but also of free speech, economic opportunity and even faith itself. And they wanted viewers to know that he was not anti-immigrant or sexist.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-5298104c-81c8-4a22-af1d-23823869871b"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-577cfa39-5806-4396-ae24-cdf4ba406715"/>
