	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-dd51e7b1-27a1-462c-af0b-237cd2866e7d">
		<rdfs:label>article-dd51e7b1-27a1-462c-af0b-237cd2866e7d</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Nearly a week after a mob of President Trump’s supporters laid siege to the Capitol, the president finds himself increasingly besieged: abandoned by big businesses, institutions and his own acting homeland security chief just as Washington and state capitols are bracing for renewed threats leading up to Inauguration Day.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-f800cdb3-ad0c-4b7e-81cb-a52efab2aad2"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2021-01-12"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-dd54b19a-0007-457a-b621-866ad5b10a49">
		<rdfs:label>article-dd54b19a-0007-457a-b621-866ad5b10a49</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Probably a lot fewer than you’ve been led to believe, but more than enough to make you nervous.</rdfs:comment>
