	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-f85fea22-b49b-46d0-a13a-62748df803d9">
		<rdfs:label>article-f85fea22-b49b-46d0-a13a-62748df803d9</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>Portsmouth, Ohio, was facing hard times when our reporter spent a week there leading up to Election Day 2016. She went back to find the town rebounding, and loyal to President Trump. But its residents no longer tie their fortunes to him.</rdfs:comment>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-8e169904-d65b-4511-95d0-3f904004a59b"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasAuthor rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;author-f0fadf15-fa98-455a-8292-90640f485764"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublishDate rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;date-2020-10-03"/>
		<PoliticalJournalism:hasPublisher rdf:resource="&PoliticalJournalism;NewYorkTimes"/>
	</PoliticalJournalism:Article>
	
	<PoliticalJournalism:Article rdf:about="&PoliticalJournalism;article-f861fc05-599b-4c39-b4d0-4dbd69188399">
		<rdfs:label>article-f861fc05-599b-4c39-b4d0-4dbd69188399</rdfs:label>
		<rdfs:comment>In a first, the measure legally defines greenhouse gases as pollution. That’ll make new regulations much tougher to challenge in court.</rdfs:comment>
